# The a64asm sample directory tracks its OUTPUT and ignores its INPUTS.
#
# The distinction is the whole point of the course: a number in the prose is
# a claim about bytes, so the BYTES are committed and the things that make
# the bytes are not.  A reader can check every figure in the course against
# a64dec.out, fields.txt, run1.txt and run2.txt without installing a
# cross-compiler.  A reader who wants to re-derive them runs build_samples.sh
# and needs clang with an aarch64 target and llvm-objdump-21, which not every
# machine has -- and a course whose numbers are only checkable on the machine
# that produced them is a course whose numbers are claims.
#
# So: the recorded runs are tracked, the objects are not.  .gitignore keeps
# the object files out of history; they are large, they are binary, and they
# are reproducible from two text files with one command.

# Object files, and the ELF sections the density comparison counts.  All of
# these are outputs of build_samples.sh.
*.o
*.s.tmp
corpus_O0.o
corpus_O1.o
corpus_O2.o
corpus_Os.o
density_a64_O0.o
density_a64_O1.o
density_a64_O2.o
density_a64_Os.o
density_x86_O0.o
density_x86_O1.o
density_x86_O2.o
density_x86_Os.o

# The disassembler's own listing, for the same reason: it is derived.
a64_objdump.txt

# The scratch files the measurement driver assembles.  a64dec.py writes
# _p.s/_p.o for a single-word probe, _b.s/_b.o for a batch, and _w.s/_w.o
# for a word asked about in isolation.  None of them is an input to anything.
_p.s
_p.o
_b.s
_b.o
_w.s
_w.o

# The object files for the density comparison are listed twice above on
# purpose: once by pattern and once by name.  The patterns catch a future
# level (-O3, -Ofast) and the names document what the script produces, so a
# reader who deletes one line does not silently start tracking binaries.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
